1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 So far we have been working with data, data that was considered to be deterministic.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 Even if it were estimates, we assumed it to be deterministic parameters.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 Today we are going to do something different.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 We are going to talk about sensitivity analysis in order to cope with the non-accuracy, I would say, of our data.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
 We will talk about risk under uncertainty and decision making under uncertainty.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 And we will talk about decision trees for stage decisions.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 We will see that we also have a probabilistic component there.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 And we will see case study.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 It will be case study as explained to you, not one to be solved, but there are some very interesting elements in it that makes the theory a little bit more realistic.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 Okay, sensitivity analysis. I think we can be very brief on that because we covered it already last week in the open office.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 So what is the idea? The idea is that you are not too confident in your result of your investment analysis

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 because you know that you have been using estimates for parameters, for instance for annual operating cost.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 And you want to check whether a change in value of that given parameter will affect your outcome of the analysis.

14
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 And we can work with expected values if you have a probability distribution for that parameter.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 Or we can work with scenarios like we have a pessimistic scenario saying, well, annual operating cost will double for instance,

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 or most likely it will stay the same. Or optimistic, I think they will be decreasing by 10% a year.

17
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 So that's the situation. Here you have the explanation for simulation analysis.

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 We're using a Monte Carlo sampling approach. I think most of you are familiar with that.

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 If not, let me explain things using an example.

20
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 So what is this example about? So we have a model. We have a present-word model, which is the present-word of the cash flows in minus the cash flow outs.

21
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 By the way, this example is so simple that we could work with an expected value of that present-word.

22
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 But let's go for a simulation approach. We have the cash flow in here, data cash flow out.

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 As we see, there's no deterministic value, but there's a probability.

24
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 We think that it can be 50,000 with a probability of 30%. It can be 60,000 with a probability of 40%.

25
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
 And it can be 70,000 with a probability of 30%. And also we're not sure about the cash flow out.

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 It can be 45% probability for 50,000 and 55% for 70,000.

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 How do we do the sampling? Well, what we do is we make a cumulative probability distribution.

28
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
 So here we have 30,000, 70,000. Here 45,000.

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 And then we associate random numbers between 0 and 99 with these cumulative probabilities.

30
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 So that means that here random number 1 stands for the random number that we will use to generate cash flow ins.

31
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 Random number 2 stands for the random number that we will use to generate cash flows out.

32
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 So here we have 30, so we have numbers from 0 to 29. Then we go to 70, so that's 30 to 69.

33
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 And then 70 to 99. And here in a similar fashion from 0 to 44 and 45 to 99.

34
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 Now we start generating random numbers. The first random number generated is 46.

35
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 We look at 46 is here, so we will use a cash flow in of 60,000.

36
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 The random number generated here is 81. 81 is here, so we have 70,000.

37
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 That means that cash flow in minus cash flow out gives us a present worth of minus 10,000.

38
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 Okay, we generate our next random number, 30, which is here, is again 60,000.

39
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 Next here we have 8 as a random number, so we are here. It's 50,000, so we have a plus.

40
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 So what we do basically is we make a statistical experiment.

41
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 We draw from two distributions, one for each of the cash flows and then we combine them.

42
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 So we are running experiments. So we need some design of experiments in order to have a confidence interval that is acceptable.

43
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
 So you see here, well, now here we had minus 10,000, here we have plus 10,000.

44
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 So the average is 0, but that would not be a very wise thing to say.

45
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 So we need a lot of these statistical experiments in order to have an average with a small enough deviation.

46
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 So basically that's how simulation is working.

47
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
 And we need it because here more than one parameter is changing at the same time.

48
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
 A very often used simulation also here for investment calculations.

49
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 Now risk and uncertainty, so that's what we had till now.

50
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 We had deterministic estimates for all parameters and so everything that we knew we were certain about.

51
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 There are two other situations possible, risk and uncertainty.

52
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 Risk is where we know that there is a probability distribution.

53
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
 We are not sure, but at least we know how likely things are to happen.

54
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
 But uncertainty we don't know. We just know that different outcomes are possible.

55
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 We know that energy costs can go down. We know that energy costs can go up.

56
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
 We know that energy costs can remain the same, but we have no clue whatsoever about the likelihood of each of these situations.

57
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 So put in a more strict language.

58
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:20,000
 So the probability are not known and for the identified states of nature and these states of nature is like I said, energy costs will go down, will stay the same, will go up.

59
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
 They're called state of nature because well, we have no influence on it.

60
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
 We don't know anything about it.

61
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:36,000
 As we will see, the Laplace criterion assumes that well, if you don't know, then just assume that each state is equally likely.

62
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:47,000
 But there are other approaches and these other approaches have to do with decision attitude, which can be very optimistic or very pessimistic or something in between.

63
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:57,000
 An advanced decision making modeling of decisions under uncertainty can be found in utility theory and game theory.

64
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
 They're used for policy making in government.

65
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
 They're used for supply chain management, partnership, establishment and deployment.

66
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 Maybe, you know, the one of the basic examples, the prisoners dilemma.

67
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 So there are two people in jail, the orange guy and the blue one.

68
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
 If they both confess, they will have a sentence of five years.

69
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,000
 If they both remain silent, they will have a sentence of one year.

70
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 But if one of them confesses and the other remains silent, the one confessing will get out and the other one will have a 20 year sentence.

71
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:43,000
 So it's really a dilemma because as a decision maker, you really don't know what the other will be doing.

72
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,000
 So that makes it a little bit awkward to decide.

73
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 Now, let's have a look at these approaches.

74
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
 So this is the matrix where we start from.

75
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
 We have the actions.

76
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
 So that's the choices that we have to make.

77
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:06,000
 For instance, investment alternative, they're numbered A1 over Ai to Am.

78
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:13,000
 We have the states of nature, theta j with j going from 1 to n.

79
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 That's what can happen.

80
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
 We don't know anything about the chances.

81
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,000
 We can't influence anything.

82
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
 It's nature if you want that decides.

83
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,000
 And then, of course, we have consequences of our actions.

84
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 If we take a given action and one of the states of nature actually happens, we will have an outcome.

85
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:38,000
 In an investment situation, it could be we will have a profit or we will have a loss.

86
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 So that's the situation we have.

87
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
 Now, how do we take decisions?

88
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:48,000
 Well, these are the criteria that are mostly used.

89
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 You see, the last one is the Laplace.

90
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
 In sufficient reason, Laplace says, well, if you don't know anything, then just assume that there are equal probabilities.

91
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,000
 And then you see the maxi min, the maxi max and the mini max.

92
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 So Walt, Hurwitz and Savage.

93
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 You see the formulas.

94
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:11,000
 On this slide, I have the explanation.

95
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:18,000
 But let's maybe I think it's easier if we have a look at the example in order to understand.

96
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
 So, OK, we have a simple example.

97
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,000
 It's chosen.

98
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 It's a virtual example.

99
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:42,000
 It's chosen because it's illustrating a specific thing about these criteria, namely, if you look at these criteria, they all indicate in other action as being the most interesting one to do.

100
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
 OK, so then one could, of course, ask, well,

101
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
 does it make sense then to use these criteria?

102
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:55,000
 And the answer is yes, they have some advantages.

103
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:01,000
 One advantage is transparency.

104
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
 Transparency or consistency.

105
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
 You indicate why you chose the action that you chose.

106
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:20,000
 It's not like, well, if we don't know, I feel like A2 is a good action or A3 is an action.

107
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:24,000
 No, you explain what your reasoning behind it was.

108
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
 So that's consistency.

109
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,000
 And the second thing is that you can.

110
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
 And that's something we will also see in multi criteria decision making.

111
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
 You can include the decision makers attitude.

112
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
 And how can we do this?

113
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:48,000
 Well, we can do that by, for instance, going for if you don't know, take equal probabilities.

114
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:57,000
 If you don't know, assume the worst, be very pessimistic or, well, always look at the bright side, take an optimistic criteria.

115
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 So these things bring transparency, they bring consistency, they allow you to bring in your own decision making attitudes.

116
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
 Now, OK, let's have a look at the example.

117
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,000
 So Laplace, as I told you, we will assume equal probabilities.

118
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:21,000
 So the expected outcome if we take action one will be one fourth of two plus two plus zero plus one is five over four.

119
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:27,000
 Here we have one fourth of one plus one plus one plus one, which is one, etc.

120
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
 And then we see that, well, the largest expected outcome is here.

121
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
 So we will pick action one.

122
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 OK, let's go to wealth here, wealth maximum.

123
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
 What are we doing?

124
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
 Well, we are computing something called a security level.

125
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
 And what is the security level?

126
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:56,000
 Well, the security level is defined as under action I, what is the worst possible return or consequence that can occur for the decision maker?

127
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:06,000
 So we are going, we're looking if we take, for instance, action A, then what is the worst possible state of nature that could happen?

128
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
 And well, what would be the worst outcome?

129
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
 That's zero.

130
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
 Here it doesn't make any difference.

131
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,000
 Here, what is the worst thing that can happen?

132
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:14,000
 That's zero.

133
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
 What is the worst thing that can happen here?

134
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
 That's zero.

135
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
 So we have the security level.

136
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:24,000
 And then wealth is saying, well, pick the one with the highest security level.

137
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
 So that's why it's called a maximin.

138
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:32,000
 We are taking for computing the security level, we take a minimum.

139
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
 Then to pick our action, we take a max.

140
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:36,000
 So that's the maximin.

141
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:41,000
 OK, next is Hurwitz.

142
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:48,000
 Hurwitz is basically the basic Hurwitz is based on the optimism level.

143
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
 And what is the optimism level?

144
00:12:50,000 --> 00:13:02,000
 Well, under action I, the optimism level is the best, not the worst, but the best possible return or consequence that can occur for the decision maker.

145
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
 So as you see here, we will pick the max.

146
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
 So here the max is two, the max is one, the max is four, the max is three.

147
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
 And we pick the one with the highest.

148
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
 So that is a three.

149
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:20,000
 There is a possibility to have a variation on that maxi max.

150
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 And we use user preference and we will introduce a parameter alpha.

151
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:34,000
 And alpha will be used to obtain a weighted average of optimism and security level.

152
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
 Like here, if you look at this one.

153
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:45,000
 So we have alpha times zero plus one minus alpha times two.

154
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:52,000
 For the second action, we have alpha times one plus one minus alpha times one.

155
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,000
 Sorry.

156
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:58,000
 So we compute a weighted average in here.

157
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
 Well, it depends a little bit on the value for alpha that you set.

158
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:11,000
 But if you set alpha less than three over four, it still will be the A3S outcome.

159
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
 So here we are optimistic.

160
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:24,000
 Here we can make a mix according to our own attitudes, whether we want to be more optimistic or more pessimistic.

161
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
 And then the last is Savage.

162
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
 Savage is a minimax regret.

163
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
 It's a little bit more complicated to compute.

164
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
 What is the regret?

165
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
 Let's first focus on the regrets.

166
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:40,000
 The ones in green, I try to tear apart the different steps.

167
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,000
 So to make it more clear.

168
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
 So what is a regret?

169
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,000
 The regret is a difference between the value resulting from the best action.

170
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:55,000
 Given that that state of nature theta J is a true state of nature.

171
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:01,000
 And the value of the resulting from action AI under theta J.

172
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
 Let's have a look.

173
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:05,000
 So what do we do?

174
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:11,000
 Well, what we do is we have a look first here.

175
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 We try to have for a given state of nature, this would be the best action.

176
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
 So let's underline.

177
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,000
 So that's what I did here.

178
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:18,000
 Or that you see here.

179
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,000
 The best possible if you have this one, the best possible would be two.

180
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
 If you have this state, the best possible would be four.

181
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
 If you have this state, the best possible would be one.

182
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:31,000
 If you have this state, the best possible one would be either one of those.

183
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,000
 What we then do is, so we have that.

184
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:40,000
 We need to compute our regret.

185
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:49,000
 So we have from this max, we have to subtract the, I would say the current one.

186
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:53,000
 So that means that here we will have, here we have two.

187
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
 So two minus two.

188
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,000
 Here we have two minus one, two minus zero, two minus one.

189
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:05,000
 Here four minus two, four minus one, four minus four, four minus three.

190
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
 You understand what is happening.

191
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:17,000
 Then from these numbers, we have to take the max.

192
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,000
 What is the max?

193
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
 So we will take a circle.

194
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:24,000
 So the maximum regret here is two, it's three.

195
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
 Here it's two and here we have one.

196
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:32,000
 And then we want to minimize the maximum regret.

197
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:37,000
 So then if you look here, the maximum regret is two, three, two, one.

198
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
 So this seems to be the most appropriate action.

199
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:45,000
 So it's not difficult, but it becomes a little bit messy.

200
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:49,000
 But that's what is decision making under uncertainty.

